<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Hitu</h1><p class="st-meta">1470 CE – 1646 CE (approx.) · capital Hitu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Muslim clove-trading state on the north coast of Ambon, whose long war with the Dutch East India Company ended in 1646.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hitu was a federation of villages on the northern peninsula of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambon-island">Ambon Island</a>, led by four chiefs called perdana. Its chronicle, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikayat-tanah-hitu">Hikayat Tanah Hitu</a> written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijali">Imam Rijali</a> in the mid-17th century, traces its rulers to settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a>. Hitu's leaders accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the late 15th century through ties with Javanese ports and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/giri-kedaton">Giri Kedaton</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Hitu grew rich selling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a> to Javanese and Malay merchants and kept close links with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ternate-sultanate">Ternate</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gowa-sultanate">Makassar</a>. Its council of chiefs governed through village heads. In 1600 Hitu allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> against the Portuguese, but soon resisted the Company's attempts to monopolise the clove trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Hitu war, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kakiali">Kakiali</a> until his murder in 1643, ended in 1646 when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> took the mountain fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapahaha">Kapahaha</a>. Hitu's leaders were absorbed into the colonial village system, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikayat-tanah-hitu">Hikayat Tanah Hitu</a> remains a key Malay source for Moluccan history.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1470 CE – 1646 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Maritime Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:88.96%;width:3.50%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1470 CE – 1646 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hitu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kakiali</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay, Hitu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hitu Kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1558&amp;focus=hitu" aria-label="Hitu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8237 2312 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hitu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8557 2568L7959 2663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7959 2663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8557 2568h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8557 2568h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hitu. <a href="/atlas?year=1558&amp;focus=hitu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

